\section{Distinguish the Two Graphs in Block-Based SSA}

A block-based intermediate representation has a control-flow
graph of blocks and a graph of relations between value definitions
and operand uses. An optimization that replaces an arithmetic
operand may not change any control edge. A branch rewrite can
change both graphs, for example by dropping an argument on a
deleted edge. Click and Paleczny's graph IR puts control and
value dependencies in a common object graph instead; it is an
alternative representation, not a reason to elide either relation.
The examples here use block arguments for SSA joins, as in the
MLIR model \cite{lattner2021mlir}, rather than explicit phi
instructions \cite{cytron1991ssa}.

Store a terminator's outgoing edge occurrences in semantic order.
For a conditional branch the true and false positions are not
interchangeable. A block with two destinations that happen to be
the same block still has two edge occurrences. If the two edges
pass different values to that block, a map keyed only by source
and target cannot represent the program. Give the edge occurrence
an identity of its own or key by owning terminator and successor
position under a contract forbidding position changes without
remapping. Maintain the target's predecessor index as the inverse
of those occurrences, not as a set of predecessor blocks.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lll}
\hline
Stored relation & Canonical location & Reverse lookup \\
\hline
Control transition & Terminator's ordered edge & Target's incoming edge IDs \\
Ordinary value use & Instruction operand slot & Definition's use list \\
Edge argument use & Outgoing edge argument slot & Definition's use list \\
Block membership & Block's ordered instructions & Instruction's owner link \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\section{Represent Uses Per Operand Occurrence}

The instruction \texttt{add(v,v)} has two uses of \texttt{v},
not one. Store a use record for each operand occurrence, with an
owner, an operand position within that owner, a definition, and
the position of the record in the definition's reverse list.
For a thin implementation the record can be embedded in the
operand slot; if changing the operand array moves it, the use
identifier must still resolve correctly or be rewritten as well.
A growable dense reverse list with swap-removal is efficient
when the order of uses has no semantics. Debug output and
deterministic passes should obtain stable ordering separately
if the revision history otherwise determines enumeration order.

In \Cref{fig:visual-424}, branch slots \(E_0\) and \(E_1\)
target the same block \(J\). The predecessor index lists two
edges. Each edge owns its argument use and \(J\)'s block
parameter gets the value from the edge by which control arrives.
Dropping \(E_1\) means unlinking its argument uses, even when a
test of the set of successor blocks sees no change.

