% Annotated walkthrough
\section{Change Operands Through Their Reverse Index}

\begin{verbatim}
set_use(u, new):
    old := use[u].definition
    if old == new: return
    require type_of(new) == expected_type(u)
    require new is available at u
    i := use[u].reverse_index
    list := uses_of[old]
    assert list[i] == u
    last := list.pop()
    if last != u:
        list[i] := last
        use[last].reverse_index := i
    use[u].definition := new
    use[u].reverse_index := uses_of[new].length
    uses_of[new].append(u)

replace_all_uses(old, new):
    if old == new: return
    preflight_types_and_availability(uses_of[old], new)
    reserve_uses_capacity(new, uses_of[old].length)
    while not uses_of[old].empty():
        set_use(uses_of[old].last(), new)
\end{verbatim}

The loop removes a use from the list it is draining; it doesn't
walk a cursor over a vector whose iteration order changes under
it. The preflight ensures that a type or dominance error cannot
leave only half of the uses replaced. For an ordinary operand,
availability includes definition-before-use order inside a block;
for an edge argument it is tested at the source terminator.
A replacement with no type conflict can still create a use
before its definition. This might be obvious when both are
instructions in one block and much harder to see at a merge.

Store and update both directions in one mutation API. Rewrites
that create a partially built graph should keep it private until
all endpoints, reverse links, and block argument arities are
reconciled. Split a critical edge, for instance, by creating a
new intermediary block, retargeting that exact terminator edge,
and attaching a forward edge passing the old values to the old
destination. Those values must still be available at the new
forwarding terminator; receiving and relaying intermediary block
arguments is one way to ensure this. Repointing every edge with
the same target would also change paths the rewrite did not select.

\section{Validate Authority With a Slow Reconstruction}

In a debug verifier, reconstruct incoming edge lists by visiting
terminators and reconstruct use lists by visiting every ordinary
operand and edge argument. Compare multisets of edge identities
or use identities, plus slot positions, to the maintained indexes.
Finding equal degrees is not enough: two missing occurrences
can be masked by two duplicates. If the IR also stores block
owner links, compare each against the actual block list. Run the
verification immediately after graph mutation transactions, not
only at the end of the compiler when the responsible pass is
hard to identify.

For a pass that edits infrequently, don't maintain a duplicate
index just because you can. A linear reconstruction may be
cheaper than keeping reverse links for every rare query.
Record graph versions for derived analyses: changing an
operand need not invalidate dominance, but retargeting an edge
potentially does. A handle's successful generation check says
the object is live, not that an old analysis about it remains
valid. \Cref{ch:ext-426} shows how a single removed edge can
change dominance while leaving both block handles unchanged.

\section{Exercise Multiplicity and Graph Churn Together}

Construct a terminator that passes \texttt{v} on one edge and
\texttt{w} on the other to the same join. Make a following
ordinary instruction use \texttt{v} twice, choosing a case where
the definition of \texttt{v} dominates all three uses. The reverse
index must have three distinct entries. Replace the uses of
\texttt{v} by another dominating value, take the false branch
only, and erase the now-unused definition. After the edit the
remaining edge argument must still be \texttt{w}. This fixture
checks both index directions and would fail on an endpoint-only
control map or a user-only value map.

For memory and latency measurements, distinguish definitions
with no users, the common short reverse lists, and a few hot
definitions used by hundreds of operands. An inline small-vector
reverse list saves allocations for common arities but fattens
every record, even when empty. Measuring only a high-fanout
synthetic program favors a different representation than editor
incremental lowering or mostly scalar optimization.

\section{Detach Cycles in Bulk Before Reclaiming Their Records}

Dead code removal can encounter an unreachable loop whose
instructions use one another through block arguments. Erasing
individual definitions only when their reverse lists are
empty will not reclaim the loop. First determine the full
deletion set. Prove that no use of a value defined in that
set survives, or rewrite those external uses. An unreachable
block can have an outgoing edge into a reachable join:
cutting the edge and unlinking its argument uses may remove
some of the alleged external uses without making the join
unreachable. Rebuild the surviving join's incoming list and
check it against the remaining terminators.

Once the cut is accounted for, unlink uses owned by deleted
instructions and edges, then free operands, edges, and
definitions in an order that leaves no index pointing into
the retired storage. Cycle handling is then a graph operation,
not a special case in the allocator. To test this path build
two unreachable blocks with a loop-carried parameter, one
of which also branches to a live join. After removal, scan
the join's predecessor list and the reverse uses of every
live definition. Verify both are free of handles to the
deleted set even if those numeric slots are allocated again.
